-- title:
--   Finite presentation descends along a nilpotent thickening
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ and $B'$ be commutative rings (in the same universe) and let $p : P \to B'$ be a ring homomorphism that is surjective and whose kernel $\mathrm{ker}\,p$ is a nilpotent ideal. Let $A$ be a commutative $P$-algebra that is flat as a $P$-module, and let $A'$ be a commutative $B'$-algebra. Suppose given a surjective ring homomorphism $q : A \to A'$ compatible with $p$, in the sense that the structure map $P \to A$ followed by $q$ equals $p$ followed by the structure map $B' \to A'$, and suppose that the kernel of $q$ is exactly the ideal of $A$ generated by the image of $\mathrm{ker}\,p$ under $P \to A$ (the ideal $\mathrm{ker}(p)\,A$). If $A'$ is of finite presentation as a $B'$-algebra, then $A$ is of finite presentation as a $P$-algebra. The rings $A$ and $A'$ are allowed to live in universes distinct from that of $P$ and $B'$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that finite presentation of a flat algebra descends along a nilpotent (in particular an infinitesimal) thickening of the base, the algebraic form of the fact that a flat deformation of a finitely presented algebra is again finitely presented. It is used in the construction of pullback rings realising a pushout square of rings with flat and surjective data, as in [`RingHom.exists_pullbackRing_isPushout_flat_of_isPushout_of_flat_of_surjective_of_isNilpotent`](thm.html#RingHom.exists_pullbackRing_isPushout_flat_of_isPushout_of_flat_of_surjective_of_isNilpotent).
